  "repair_or_experiment_note": "User requested the latest MoE in the original local player, then supplied a screenshot of a real startup/runtime error. This stage updates and restarts that requested RM7v7 player from the latest complete, whole-CPU-verified SCUT+Fudan seventh-hour cohort. It does not fetch intermediate eighth-hour weights or change remote training or accounting.\n\nH1 a21a82639c332456a7f89c985c838616ab5f8e5771cc71f32894485658ca866a / Adam666920, E2 dd76c55798ab0f059f168a67cb23b68f90d95fb2b09fab0d119d0ea6d5600c2c / Adam724440, and E3 6b5215aedd8ef51e8fa4b09fb85b967f0e21203289b025e182e872841c9d79cf / Adam680440 were already fully collected locally. Original frozen E0/W35 and trained gate148 normal selection remain. Public manual jump/recovery/spin choose one whole six-action expert vector. This is not a newly trained five-way gate or a new skill qualification.\n\nExported latest_moe_scut_fudan_hour7_20261019.onnx: 700 CPU rows, all public selectors and all five routed experts checked against direct PyTorch expert actions; ONNX maximum clipped-action difference 0.00005054473876953125 against bound 0.0001. Full RM7v7 original robot initialization, six-actuator action wiring and five-step normal/jump/recovery/spin smoke checks passed. Existing config, PD60/2, wheel damping .6, crank .5, no yaw assist, 31 robot bodies and 10 constraints remain. Old hour4 exports remain on disk.\n\nThe first selected local conda interpreter lacked onnxruntime and failed during import before export or player mutation. The original error is retained. The player's existing .venv already contained all dependencies and exported successfully; no environment installation was performed.\n\nFirst actual launch at 08:33:20 created run 1792370000596194400 and initially had a running viewer and fresh finite status. At 08:33:43 its simulation thread exited on real PermissionError WinError5 replacing INTERACTIVE_CURRENT.tmp with INTERACTIVE_CURRENT.json. The user screenshot, original traceback, temporary and complete snapshots and original source are retained. The original initial-success receipt is not overwritten or treated as sustained success. The launch script's console projection printed null fields for an OrderedDictionary; its saved structured receipt is populated and was inspected directly. That display artifact is distinct from the real later runtime error.\n\nClosed only that identified failed local player's window gracefully after its simulation thread had exited. Replaced status serialization with player_status_io.py: atomic complete JSON replacement, three short 2/4/8ms retries for Windows errors 5/32/33, then preserve the old complete status and log the original error while allowing the next status update to retry. Other exceptions still propagate; physics and policy error handling remain. No training lock was removed, and no remote process was stopped.\n\nA real Windows CreateFileW handle sharing READ/WRITE but denying DELETE reproduced the replacement failure three times. Each call returned within about 21ms without throwing, retained valid sequence0 JSON, and logged its original error. Closing the handle allowed the next call to publish sequence4; an unrelated missing-directory error still propagated. The source and exact results are retained. This checks actual Windows sharing behavior, not a synthetic success flag.\n\nThe status-fixed V2 player initially ran, then the user clicked follow-robot and it exited natively at about 08:36:42. Its bounded observer failed on stale status; that failure, all observed samples, original source and run snapshots remain. The user explicitly confirmed that the window exited by itself after clicking follow. PLAYER_V2_STDERR.txt contains `ERROR mjv_updateCamera (engine_vis_visualize.c:3066): track body id is outside valid range`, followed by a GLFW context cleanup warning. This is distinct from the first Python status-write failure.\n\nThe original camera code enabled TRACKING before setting trackbodyid, without the passive viewer render lock. A render thread could therefore observe the default invalid -1 body. The camera fix publishes both camera modes under viewer.lock(), validates the body id and installs it before enabling tracking. No policy or simulation parameter is changed. A real passive viewer on the unchanged full map rendered 100 consecutive follow/overview switches successfully with chassis_id1 and 32 bodies, without physics stepping. That regression uses the same helper called by the actual button and M key.\n\nThe status-and-camera-fixed V3 player is a distinct new run. See its actual launch receipt and final bounded 45-second observation for outcome and creation identity. The earlier failed observation is not overwritten or claimed passed. The graph parity still applies because both repairs affect only status I/O and camera presentation; the exported ONNX is unchanged.\n\nBoth START_WASD.bat and START_RM7V7_MOE.bat still launch the requested drive_rm7v7_moe.py, now bound to seventh-hour SCUT+Fudan experts. P starts/pauses; WASD drives; J jumps; K recovers; Z/B spins; N/X selects normal. Initial state is paused. Existing 29x16m RM7v7 terrain is retained, including its previously documented absence of base/outpost mechanisms. This local MuJoCo player remains a different backend from training and does not establish PhysX or full-course graduation.\n\nThe unused first prepared monitor-player-fact args are retained as prepared history: they preceded the user's runtime-error feedback and were not sent. The final monitor update adds the actual fixed-player fact while preserving the existing eighth-hour training identities, ACTIVE hourly recurrence and original target. Future heartbeats must preserve the user's window and must not automatically reopen or hotpatch it.\n",
  "validation": "700-row ONNX/PyTorch whole-action parity and original fullmap wiring; real Windows deny-delete contention reproduced and recovered; original first runtime failure and follow-camera native exit retained; 100 real render camera switches; V3 fresh finite status for 45s; same ACTIVE hourly monitor and target; no remote training/account changes.",
